                    coach_r1_founder:       `<strong>You're treating the founder as gone.</strong> But three of your executives still check decisions against what <em>he</em> would think, and he is the non-executive chair your board confidence runs through. You can't transform a company while pretending its founder left the room.`,
                    coach_r2_cocreate:      `<strong>Co-creating the roadmap</strong> turns the team from an audience into authors. People defend what they help build. This is the surest route from performed “yes” to genuine alignment — and it gives the President voice without conceding the strategy.`,
                    coach_r2_burning:       `<strong>A jointly-built burning platform</strong> aligns the team around a shared diagnosis of <em>why</em>, which is what sustains energy over a multi-year journey. Built jointly it lands; imposed it would just be another slide.`,
                    coach_r2_offsite:       `<strong>An off-site to contain anxieties</strong> creates the psychological holding the team needs to tolerate uncertainty. Containment isn't avoidance — it's the deliberate management of collective emotion so the work can proceed.`,
                    coach_r2_incentives:    `<strong>Realigning incentives</strong> moves behavior, but incentives shift compliance faster than belief. Useful as reinforcement; thin as a primary mechanism for emotional resistance rooted in identity.`,
                    coach_r2_remove:        `<strong>You fired the Infrastructure President in Week 3.</strong> The team is now visibly “aligned” and visibly terrified. You taught everyone that disagreement gets you removed, so the hedging went underground and the founder-chair is alarmed. Did you align the team — or silence it? Common Error #3.`,
                    coach_r2_blind:         `<strong>The mechanism is reasonable, but you chose it half-blind.</strong> Because you misdiagnosed the President's real driver, you're treating an identity wound with a process tool. The mechanism lands at a fraction of its power.`,
                    coach_r2_avoid:         `<strong>That's avoidance, not containment.</strong> Forcing nothing lets the hedging harden underground and the board clock runs. Containment holds anxiety so the work can proceed — it doesn't skip the work.`,
                    coach_r2_force:         `<strong>You forced the issue too hard.</strong> Pressure produces compliance, not alignment — the room agrees and the resistance goes underground. You bought a “yes” you'll pay for at Q2.`,
                    coach_r2_talent_empty:  `<strong>You promised the cloud leaders alignment you don't yet have.</strong> They will read the room faster than you think. Showing them a team that isn't genuinely aligned accelerates, not slows, their exit.`,
                    coach_r2_talent_wait:   `<strong>“Alignment first, talent later” is a luxury you don't have.</strong> The two cloud leaders are the transformation's engine and they're being courted now. Wait, and the engine walks out the door.`,
                    coach_r3_alignfirst:    `<strong>Alignment before anything visible</strong> is the durable sequence: with the team genuinely behind you, every later move compounds instead of fracturing. Just watch the board clock — alignment work has to show progress, not disappear.`,
                    coach_r3_winearly:      `<strong>An early win before the team is aligned</strong> builds momentum but exposes the divide: the skeptics will frame any win as proof their path was right. Sequencing matters — what had to be true first?`,
                    coach_r3_win_ok:        `<strong>An early win on a genuinely aligned team</strong> compounds: momentum and board confidence rise together because the team owns it. Well sequenced.`,
                    coach_r3_capitalfirst:  `<strong>You opened by moving SGD 180M away from the legacy unit.</strong> Decisive — but the team wasn't aligned yet, and you just handed the skeptics proof you don't understand the business. The President is now actively organizing against you. Common Error #4.`,
                    coach_r3_capital_ok:    `<strong>Capital reallocation on an aligned team</strong> reads as shared resolve, not betrayal. Because the President had voice in it, the SGD 180M moves without fracturing the team.`,
                    coach_r3_chairfirst:    `<strong>Aligning the founder-chair and family bloc first</strong> secures your board confidence at the source — but don't let it slow the team work so much that momentum stalls. Bring the chair <em>with</em> you, then move.`,
                    coach_r3_tooslow:       `<strong>You're pacing too cautiously.</strong> Readiness matters, but the board granted only two quarters. Move this slowly and you'll lose the family bloc's patience before the strategy is ever tested.`,
                    coach_r3_toofast:       `<strong>You're moving at maximum speed.</strong> Speed without readiness fractures the team and arms the skeptics. The board clock pressures you, but a fractured team delivers nothing to show for the haste.`,
                    coach_r4_triumph:       `<strong>A confident update on a genuinely aligned team</strong> is fine — the substance backs the claim. Just stay open to the chair's probing; certainty that can't be questioned reads as spin.`,
                    coach_r4_triumph_caught:`<strong>You overclaimed and the chair found the gap.</strong> Declaring the team “fully aligned” when it's still partly compliant invites exactly the probe you can't survive. Honest progress would have held more confidence than a triumph you can't defend.`,
                    coach_r4_defensive:     `<strong>Blaming the board's timeline</strong> tells the lead director you can't deliver and won't own it. Contain the board's anxiety — don't return it to them. Defensiveness reads as a CEO already losing the room.`,
                    coach_r4_invite_real:   `<strong>You invited the Infrastructure President to speak — and he spoke <em>for</em> the transformation in his own words.</strong> Nothing demonstrates genuine alignment like your loudest former skeptic making the case. The chair's anxiety just dropped.`,
                    coach_r4_invite_exposed:`<strong>You invited the President to speak before the alignment was real.</strong> In the room, in front of the chair, the gap showed. Inviting him was the right instinct — you just hadn't done the work that makes it safe.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>Deflecting to the metrics</strong> tells the founder-chair you'd rather not answer the question he actually asked. He notices. Avoiding the alignment question is how you confirm the alignment doubt.`,
                    coach_r4_nocontain:     `<strong>You left the board to stew.</strong> Transformation provokes anxiety in boards too. Hold them deliberately — a board you don't contain fills the silence with its own worst case.`,

                    sum_default:            `Move made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Read identity beneath the argument; saw the surface “yes.”`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Misread the driver or took the “yes” at face value.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Built genuine alignment by co-creation / shared diagnosis.`,
                    sum_r2_fear:            `Aligned by fear — removed the skeptic.`,
